            %p
          %h3#the_symmetric_group__2
            The symmetric group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_4
          %p
            The symmetric group on 4 elements is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
            to the full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tetrahedral+group"} tetrahedral group
            as well as to the orientation-preserving
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octahedral+group"} octahedral group
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#cycle_decomposition Cycle decomposition
          %p
            As an element of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+group"} symmetric group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma : X \to X
            generates a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+group"} cyclic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \sigma \rangle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+action"} group action
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbits"} orbits
            of this action partition the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            into a disjoint union of cycles, called the
            %strong cyclic decomposition
            of the permutation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %p
            For example, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            be the permutation on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 6
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (6)
            defined by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ↦
                          %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mn 2
                          %mo ↦
                          %mn 4
                        %mtd
                          %mn 3
                          %mo ↦
                          %mn 5
                        %mtd
                          %mn 4
                          %mo ↦
                          %mn 6
                        %mtd
                          %mn 5
                          %mo ↦
                          %mn 3
                        %mtd
                          %mn 6
                          %mo ↦
                          %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma = (\array{1 \mapsto 1 &amp; 2 \mapsto 4 &amp; 3 \mapsto 5 &amp; 4 \mapsto 6 &amp; 5 \mapsto 3 &amp; 6 \mapsto 2})
          %p
            The domain of the permutation is partitioned into three
            = succeed "-orbits" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle\sigma\rangle
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 6
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ∪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 4
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 6
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ∪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 3
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 5
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (6) = \{1\} \cup \{2,4,6\} \cup \{3,5\}
          %p corresponding to the three cycles
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %munder
                    %mo →
                    %mi σ
                  %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mn 2
                  %munder
                    %mo →
                    %mi σ
                  %mn 4
                  %munder
                    %mo →
                    %mi σ
                  %mn 6
                  %munder
                    %mo →
                    %mi σ
                  %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mn 3
                  %munder
                    %mo →
                    %mi σ
                  %mn 5
                  %munder
                    %mo →
                    %mi σ
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  1 \underset{\sigma}{\to} 1 \qquad
                  2 \underset{\sigma}{\to} 4 \underset{\sigma}{\to} 6 \underset{\sigma}{\to} 2 \qquad
                  3 \underset{\sigma}{\to} 5 \underset{\sigma}{\to} 3
          %p
            We can express this more compactly by writing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            in “cycle notation”, as the composition
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mn 4
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mn 6
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 3
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mn 5
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma = (1)(2\,4\,6)(3\,5)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mn 4
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mn 6
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 3
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mn 5
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma = (2\,4\,6)(3\,5)
            leaving implicit the action of the identity (1).
          %p
            Even in the simple example of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_3
            one can see some patterns.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 12
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (12)
                clearly has order 2, whilst
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 123
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (123)
                has square
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 132
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (132)
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 123
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (123)
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 123
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 12
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 13
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (123) = (12)(13)
                , so transpositions generate the whole group.
          %p
            This gives a listing of the elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_3
            as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 12
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 13
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 23
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 123
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 132
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ 1, (12),(13),(23),(123),(132)\}.
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_3
              has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+presentation"} presentation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mn 3
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi b
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mi b
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle a,b | a^3, b^2,(a b)^2 \rangle.
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            corresponds to the 3-cycle,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 123
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (123)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            corresponds to any transposition. It also has a presentation in which the generators correspond to the transpositions:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msubsup
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 2
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \sigma_1,\sigma_2 | \sigma_1^2,\sigma_2^2, (\sigma_1\sigma_2)^3\rangle
          %p It is easy to see that these relations imply that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_1\sigma_2\sigma_1= \sigma_2\sigma_1\sigma_2
          %p
            which relates this symmetric group to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group"} braid group
            = succeed "," do
              %strong Br 3
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trefoil+knot"} trefoil group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 3
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Br
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_3\to Br_3
            obtained by making the two braid generators become the transpositions.
          %h3#ConjugacyClasses Conjugacy classes
          #ConjugacycClassesOfSymmetricGroupCorrespondToCycleSet.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjugacy+classes"} conjugacy classes
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+group"} symmetric group
                labeled by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partitions"} partitions
                by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycle"} cycle
                lengths)
              %br/
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma(n)
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+group"} symmetric group
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              elements. Then the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjugacy+classes"} conjugacy classes
              of elements of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma(n)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutations"} permutations
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              elements, correspond to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycle"} cycle
              structures: two elements are conjugate to each other precisely if they have the same number
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo #
                    %mi cycles
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \# cycles \in \mathbb{N}
              of distinct
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycles"} cycles
              of the same lengths
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi l
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ≥
                      %msub
                        %mi l
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ≥
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ≥
                      %msub
                        %mi l
                        %mrow
                          %mo #
                          %mi cycles
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (l_1 \geq l_2 \geq \cdots \geq l_{\#cycles})
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition"} partition
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi l
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi l
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi l
                      %mrow
                        %mo #
                        %mi cycles
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    l_1 + l_2 + \cdots + l_{\# cycles}
                    \\;=\;
                    n
            %p
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Sagan01"} Sagan 01, p. 3 (18 of 254)
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p For the symmetric group on three elements there are three such classes:
            %div
              %div{:style => "float:left;width:30%;margin:10%"}
                %div{:style => "padding-bottom:20px"} (1 2 3) ~ (1 3 2)
                %div{:style => "padding-bottom:20px"} (1 2)(3) ~ (1 3)(2) ~ (1)(2 3)
                %div (1)(2)(3)
              %div{:style => "float:left;width:20%"}
                %div
                  %svg{:height => "40", "se:nonce" => "39384", :width => "80", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:se" => "http://svg-edit.googlecode.com", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
                    %desc Young diagram (3)
                    %g
                      %title Layer 1
                      %rect#svg_39384_1{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "10", :y => "10"}
                      %rect#svg_39384_2{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "30", :y => "10"}
                      %rect#svg_39384_3{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "50", :y => "10"}
                %div
                  %svg{:height => "60", "se:nonce" => "39384", :width => "60", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:se" => "http://svg-edit.googlecode.com", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
                    %desc Young diagram (2,1)
                    %g
                      %title Layer 1
                      %rect#svg_39384_1{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "10", :y => "10"}
                      %rect#svg_39384_2{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "30", :y => "10"}
                      %rect#svg_39384_3{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "10", :y => "30"}
                %div
                  %svg{:height => "90", "se:nonce" => "39384", :width => "40", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:se" => "http://svg-edit.googlecode.com", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
                    %desc Young diagram (1,1,1)
                    %g
                      %title Layer 1
                      %rect#svg_39384_1{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "10", :y => "10"}
                      %rect#svg_39384_2{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "10", :y => "30"}
                      %rect#svg_39384_3{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "10", :y => "50"}
              %div{:style => "float:left;width:20%"}
                %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/The3SheetedCoveringsOfTheCircle.png", :width => "100"}/
          %div{:style => "width:200px;height:30px"}
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %hr/
          %h3#representation_theory Representation theory
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory+of+the+symmetric+group"} representation theory of the symmetric group
          %h3#relation_to_the_field_with_one_element Relation to the field with one element
          %p
            One may regard the symmetric group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+linear+group"} general linear group
            in dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+with+one+element"} field with one element
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi GL
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GL(n,\mathbb{F}_1)
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "field+with+one+element#AlgebraOverF1"} there
          %h3#ClassifyingSpaceAndThomSpace Classifying space and universal Thom space
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \Sigma(n)
            of the symmetric group on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            elements may be presented by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Emb
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi Σ
